articles.content_en:
Article 1: Where any person produces, sells, or provides counterfeit drugs under any of the following circumstances, a severe punishment shall be imposed as the circumstances warrant:
(1) The drugs involved are mainly intended for use by pregnant women, puerperae, children, or critically ill patients;
(2) The drugs involved are narcotic drugs, psychotropic substances, toxic drugs for medical use, radioactive drugs, biological products, or other drugs are counterfeited with precursor chemicals used in the production of drugs;
(3) The drugs involved are injection drugs or emergency drugs;
(4) The drugs involved are used for responding to emergencies such as natural disasters, accident disasters, public health incidents, or social security incidents;
(5) Drug use units and their staff produce or sell counterfeit drugs;
(6) Other circumstances where a severe punishment shall be imposed as the circumstances warrant.

Article 2: Where any person produces, sells, or provides counterfeit drugs under any of the following circumstances, it shall be deemed as 'causing serious harm to human health' as prescribed in Article 141 of the Criminal Law:
(1) Causing minor injury or serious injury;
(2) Causing mild disability or moderate disability;
(3) Causing organ or tissue damage resulting in general dysfunction or severe dysfunction;
(4) Other circumstances that cause serious harm to human health.

Article 3: Where any person produces, sells, or provides counterfeit drugs under any of the following circumstances, it shall be deemed as 'other serious circumstances' as prescribed in Article 141 of the Criminal Law:
(1) Causing a relatively large public health emergency;
(2) The amount involved in producing, selling, or providing counterfeit drugs is 200,000 yuan or more but less than 500,000 yuan;
(3) The amount involved in producing, selling, or providing counterfeit drugs is 100,000 yuan or more but less than 200,000 yuan, and any circumstance specified in Article 1 of this Interpretation is present;
(4) The circumstances shall be deemed as serious based on factors such as the time, quantity, type of counterfeit drugs, and degree of harm to human health.

Article 4: Where any person produces, sells, or provides counterfeit drugs under any of the following circumstances, it shall be deemed as 'other especially serious circumstances' as prescribed in Article 141 of the Criminal Law:
(1) Causing severe disability or above;
(2) Causing serious injury, moderate disability, or organ or tissue damage resulting in severe dysfunction to three or more persons;
(3) Causing mild disability or organ or tissue damage resulting in general dysfunction to five or more persons;
(4) Causing minor injury to ten or more persons;
(5) Causing a major or especially major public health emergency;
(6) The amount involved in producing, selling, or providing counterfeit drugs is 500,000 yuan or more;
(7) The amount involved in producing, selling, or providing counterfeit drugs is 200,000 yuan or more but less than 500,000 yuan, and any circumstance specified in Article 1 of this Interpretation is present;
(8) The circumstances shall be deemed as especially serious based on factors such as the time, quantity, type of counterfeit drugs, and degree of harm to human health.

Article 7: Where any person commits an act obstructing drug administration under any of the following circumstances, it shall be deemed as 'sufficient to seriously endanger human health' as prescribed in Article 142a of the Criminal Law:
(1) Producing or selling drugs prohibited from use by the drug regulatory department of the State Council, and considering the time, quantity, reasons for prohibition, and other circumstances, it is deemed that there is a real danger of serious harm to human health;
(2) Producing drugs without obtaining relevant approval documents for drugs, or knowingly selling the aforesaid drugs, and the drugs involved fall under the circumstances specified in subparagraphs (1) to (3) of Article 1 of this Interpretation;
(3) Producing drugs without obtaining relevant approval documents for drugs, or knowingly selling the aforesaid drugs, and the indications, functions, or ingredients of the drugs involved are unclear;
(4) Producing drugs without obtaining relevant approval documents for drugs, or knowingly selling the aforesaid drugs, and the drugs involved have no national drug standard or approved drug quality standard, but chemical drug ingredients are detected;
(5) Importing drugs without obtaining relevant approval documents for drugs, or knowingly selling the aforesaid drugs, and the drugs involved are not legally marketed abroad;
(6) Intentionally using false test drugs in drug non-clinical studies or drug clinical trials, or failing to report serious adverse events related to drugs for clinical trials;
(7) Intentionally destroying original drug non-clinical study data or drug clinical trial data, or fabricating animal subject information, human subject information, main trial process records, research data, test data, or other drug non-clinical study data or drug clinical trial data, thereby affecting the safety, efficacy, and quality controllability of the drugs;
(8) Fabricating production or inspection records, thereby affecting the safety, efficacy, and quality controllability of the drugs;
(9) Other circumstances sufficient to seriously endanger human health.
Whether the drugs involved are legally marketed abroad shall be determined according to law based on evidence such as certificates from foreign drug regulatory authorities or rights holders, combined with evidence materials provided by the criminal suspect, defendant, and their defenders, through comprehensive review.
Where it is difficult to determine whether it is 'sufficient to seriously endanger human health', a determination shall be made based on the expert opinion issued by the drug regulatory authority at or above the prefecture level, combined with other evidence.

Article 8: Where any person commits an act obstructing drug administration under any of the circumstances specified in Article 2 of this Interpretation, it shall be deemed as 'causing serious harm to human health' as prescribed in Article 142a of the Criminal Law.
Where any person commits an act obstructing drug administration that is sufficient to seriously endanger human health, and also falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall be deemed as 'having other serious circumstances' as prescribed in Article 142a of the Criminal Law:
(1) Producing or selling drugs prohibited from use by the drug regulatory department of the State Council, with the amount of production or sale being 500,000 yuan or more;
(2) Producing or importing drugs without obtaining relevant approval documents, or knowingly selling the aforesaid drugs, with the amount of production or sale being 500,000 yuan or more;
(3) Providing false certificates, data, materials, samples, or resorting to other deceptive means in the application for drug registration, causing serious consequences;
(4) Fabricating production or inspection records, causing serious consequences;
(5) Causing adverse social impact or other circumstances of a serious nature.
Where an act prescribed in Article 142a of the Criminal Law also constitutes the crime of producing, selling, or providing counterfeit drugs, the crime of producing, selling, or providing substandard drugs, or any other crime, the conviction and punishment shall be in accordance with the provisions for the heavier punishment.

Article 9: Where any person, knowing that another person is committing a crime endangering drug safety, falls under any of the following circumstances, the person shall be treated as a joint offender:
(1) Providing funds, loans, accounts, invoices, certificates, or licenses;
(2) Providing production or business premises, equipment, or convenient conditions such as transportation, storage, custody, mailing, or sales channels;
(3) Providing production technology or raw materials, excipients, packaging materials, labels, or instructions;
(4) Providing false drug non-clinical study reports, drug clinical trial reports, or related materials;
(5) Providing advertising or promotion;
(6) Providing any other assistance.

Article 10: In handling criminal cases involving the production, sale, or provision of counterfeit drugs, the production, sale, or provision of substandard drugs, obstruction of drug administration, or other related crimes, the subjective intent of the actor shall be determined based on a comprehensive assessment of facts such as the actor's professional experience, cognitive ability, drug quality, procurement channels and prices, sales channels and prices, and production or sale methods. Where any of the following circumstances exists, the actor may be found to have the subjective intent to commit the relevant crime, unless there is evidence proving that the actor genuinely lacks such intent:
(1) The drug price is obviously different from the market price;
(2) Purchasing drugs from a producer or seller without the required qualifications, and failing to provide lawful and valid proof of origin;
(3) Evading or resisting supervision or inspection;
(4) Transferring, concealing, or destroying the drugs involved or the records of purchases and sales;
(5) Having previously been punished for committing illegal or criminal acts endangering drug safety, and subsequently committing similar acts;
(6) Other circumstances sufficient to establish the actor's subjective intent.
